Z||000000||WisconsinGT||03-12-2000||11:39 PM||||Last summer I put in a new k&N filter. Today I took it out to see if I need to clean it. It looked the same as it did when I took it out of the box. SO I am wondering if this is a sign that something is not quite right whit my air intake. I do notice a sucking sound when I start the car in the morning. But perhaps something is restricting the flow. What do you think?<P>I has new rotors and pads put on all fours. Had calipers installed on the back. The guy said that I need to get a different right rear caliper. Something to do with the E-brake not being able to work properly. Does this make sense to anyone? Could it be the nut behind the wrench? <P>While the car was on the hoist, I noticed that I have a cap (Don't know the proper name of it.) missing on the drivers side floor. I poked my finger into the area and found out that it was the carpet. If anyone can figure out what I am saying, is it possible to get one of these? If you trun to page 134 of your Haynes manual you can see what I am talking about in picture 5.7. On the lower right hand side, below the letter "B" is what I am referring to. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||kupop87gt||03-13-2000||12:07 AM||||Well, you see, the K&N filter doesn't trap anything. =) Just kidding, although I hear it's filteration isn't very good. Check the intake tract for crud. It was oiled properly when it was put in right?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||03-13-2000||12:17 AM||||I've been happy with my K&N air filter(s).  Just keep them clean and oiled.<P>For the brakes, the e-brake is a little tricky, could have failed through corrosion. Hard to tell from this distance if the repair was really needed.  Incidentally, ALWAYS use your e-brake, even with an automatic. They commonly fail through disuse. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||03-13-2000||12:38 AM||||Yes, a special caliper is needed for the E-brake to work.  It has a lever and a screw to mechanically hold the piston against the pad when the cable is pulled.||||reg||12
